the formal name for france today is the  fifth french republic  the fifth which as i'm sure you'll be  aware is more republics than most  nations go through  this factoid raises the obvious question  how  how has france gone through so many  republics so the only way to find this  out is by looking at why the first full  republics failed and what led to the  french continuously implementing new  ones  so the first french republic the one  with all the guillotining aka the fun  one was brought into being in 1792  after the french revolution three years  earlier it was to put it mildly pretty  unstable and it saw several forms of  republican government ending in the  consulate  whereby three men consuls led by the  first council napoleon bonaparte ran the  country as a dictatorship  in 1804 napoleon proclaimed the  establishment of the french empire with  himself as its emperor  that's republic number one dead  napoleon's rule would come to an end in  1815 when all of europe barring poland  united against him  and after he was ousted came the bourbon  restoration which brought back the old  bull bomb royal family with louis the  18th as france's new constitutional  monarch  he ruled for about a decade before  catching a mild case of death and he was  succeeded by charles the tenth  charles wasn't too keen on being a  constitutional monarch and so he tried  to make himself an absolute one  this didn't sit well with the french  people revolution and charles was  replaced by a constitutional monarch  louis philippe  now louis philippe did respect the  limits on his power but he unfortunately  presided over a decline in french living  standards and so  revolution this revolt part of the  broader 1848 revolution saw the creation  of a presidential republic which then  elected a man called louis napoleon as  president  now the french constitution prevented  the president from seeking re-election  which louis napoleon didn't like and so  he did what all capable men would he  overthrew the government in 1851 and  declared the establishment of a second  french empire with himself ruling as  napoleon iii  that's republic number two dead napoleon  reigned until 1870 when he made the  silly mistake of picking a fight with  prussia  prussia won napoleon was captured and  during his captivity members of  parliament declared him deposed in the  institution of another french republic  now the third french republic was a  successful one and despite a deeply  divided electorate it managed to survive  until 1940 when france fell to germany  in the second world war  most of it was occupied in the bits that  remained free from occupation became the  french state led by philippe patom  that's republic number three dead as  you'll know many in france opposed baton  and the three french forces in exile  would end up regaining control of the  country in 1945 when it was liberated by  the allies there was a nationwide  referendum the next year which approved  the creation of a fourth republic  now the fourth republic was a bit  different the french parliament and  prime minister had immense power whereas  the president had very little  the fourth republic also attempted to  bring many of france's colonies into  france itself  most preferred independence and as the  french empire disintegrated it was only  algeria which became a formal part of  france  now the fourth republic was frankly a  hot mess political divisions were  extreme and parliament was in complete  deadlock  and this deadlock caused problems for  the french military since the constant  changes in policy meant that they  couldn't secure algeria and the rest of  the french empire  this upset them and so to stop algeria  from falling away they did the only  thing they could in 1958  they staged a coup this saw parts of the  french military seize control of algeria  and soon afterwards corsica  president renee cotty declared that the  fourth republic had failed and that  civil war was inevitable unless charles  de gaulle returned as president  de gaulle was basically instated as a  dictator for six months with near  unlimited power and during the period he  oversaw the creation of a new  constitution  in september of 1958 de gaulle put this  new constitution to the people in an  empire-wide referendum which was  overwhelmingly backed  the fourth republic was dead long live  the fifth republic  i hope you enjoyed this episode and a  special thanks to my patrons  james bissonette the pastry section  danny maloney marvin cassell kelly  moneymaker rob waterhouse john bisquez  mo james castaneda aaron the whites  jordan longley marcus arsna  gustav swan jerry lamdin john bailey  calling castleman rasheed ali spinning  three plates filled  oink oink maggie patzkowski david  silverman izzy  lexi schwinn robert wetzel skye  chappelle spencer lightfoot and winston  caywood
 

